  <h1>Bin Stores, Outlet Stores, and Thrift Stores: What Is the Difference?</h1>
  <p class="lead">Four kinds of discount store are easy to confuse, because all of them sell cheap goods in plain buildings: the bin store, the outlet store run by a retailer, the thrift store, and the thrift outlet where goods are sold from bins by weight. They differ in where the goods come from, how the price is set, and how much checking the goods get before sale. This page sets the four side by side, in plain terms.</p>

  <h2>The bin store: returns, sold by the item</h2>
  <p>A bin store buys returned goods and overstock from big retailers, in bulk pallets, and sells them from large bins at one flat price per item. The price usually steps down day by day through a weekly cycle that starts on restock day. Nothing is individually priced, tested, or guaranteed; the goods are sold as they are, and many sales are final. The stock is mostly new or once-opened merchandise that passed through a big retailer's returns system, which is why bin stores are often called Amazon bin stores or returns stores. The full model is explained in the <a href="/guide/">guide to how bin stores work</a>, and the pricing cycle in the <a href="/bin-store-prices/">guide to bin store prices</a>.</p>

  <h2>The thrift outlet: donations, sold by weight</h2>
  <p>A thrift outlet, most often a Goodwill outlet, is the end of the line for donated goods. Donations that did not sell in ordinary thrift stores, or that arrived in too much volume to process, are rolled out in large bins and sold by the pound, with different per-pound rates for different categories such as clothing, hard goods, and books. Shoppers dig through unsorted bins that are rotated through the day. The goods are donations, not retail returns, so the mix runs older and more worn than a bin store's, and the by-weight price makes clothing and light goods especially cheap and heavy goods a calculation. Two listings in this directory, in Atlanta and Nashville, are Goodwill outlets; they sit in the bin store world by format, but their goods and pricing follow this donation model, and their listings say so.</p>

  <h2>The retail outlet store: one brand, normal shelves</h2>
  <p>A retail outlet store is run by a brand or a chain to sell its own unsold stock: last season's clothing, overproduced lines, and slightly irregular goods. It looks like an ordinary store, with shelves, racks, price tags on each item, fitting rooms, and normal return policies or something close to them. Prices are lower than the brand's main stores, but each item is priced for what it is, and the goods have been through the brand's own quality control. Outlet malls gather many of these in one place. The difference from a bin store is total: a known brand, known goods, individual prices, and no digging. A shopper who wants a specific brand's product cheaper goes to an outlet store; a shopper who wants to hunt through unknown goods goes to a bin store.</p>

  <h2>The thrift store: donations, sold by the item</h2>
  <p>An ordinary thrift store sells donated goods, each one sorted, hung or shelved, and given its own price tag by the store's staff. Because each item is handled and priced, thrift store prices vary by item and reflect the staff's judgment of condition and value. The goods are used, donated by households rather than returned to retailers, and the range covers clothing, furniture, housewares, books, and whatever else a neighborhood gives away. Thrift stores usually allow no returns either, but the goods were at least looked at by a person before pricing, which bin store goods were not.</p>

  <h2>The four, side by side</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bin store:</strong> retail returns and overstock; sold from bins; one flat price per item that drops through the week; unchecked, sold as it is.</li>
    <li><strong>Thrift outlet (Goodwill outlet):</strong> donated goods at the end of the thrift pipeline; sold from bins; priced by the pound; unchecked, sold as it is.</li>
    <li><strong>Retail outlet store:</strong> one brand's own unsold stock; sold from shelves and racks; each item tagged; quality controlled by the brand.</li>
    <li><strong>Thrift store:</strong> donated goods; sorted and shelved; each item tagged by staff; used goods, briefly checked.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The shared thread is that all four exist because the ordinary retail system produces more goods than it can sell at full price, and households own more than they keep. The difference is which surplus each one handles, and how much work is done on the goods before they reach the floor. The less work a store does on its goods, the cheaper and the riskier the shopping. Bin Stores USA lists the first kind, bin stores, with the occasional thrift outlet where its format belongs to the same hunt; start from the <a href="/">city list on the home page</a>.</p>
